            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't see any reference to a Redfish schema, nor a reference in the design document to bmcweb.  Please fix this.  If this is an OEM schema spell that out."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Provisioning/Provisioning.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "A new top-level \"provisioning\" directory is not acceptable.  Place this under Control maybe?  State?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Provisioning/Provisioning.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "That means ... this BMC is \"provisioned\"?  What does it mean to be provisioned?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Provisioning/Provisioning.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Abhilash Raju",
                        "email": "abhilash.kollam@gmail.com",
                        "username": "abhilashraju"
                    },
                    "message": "The term \"provisioned\" in this context signifies an operational status where:\n 1) The BMC has been successfully authenticated and securely integrated into the trusted management network.\n 2) This specific BMC is now able to  provision other (un-provisioned) BMCs."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Provisioning/Provisioning.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Chinmay Shripad Hegde",
                        "email": "hosmanechinmay@gmail.com",
                        "username": "chinmay-hegde"
                    },
                    "message": "Are you planning to use SPDM 1.2 Certificate provisioning from primary BMC to peer BMC for provisioning?. If it's just for attestation then should we call this as attestation instead where primary BMC is acting as a verifier?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Provisioning/Provisioning.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Abhilash Raju",
                        "email": "abhilash.kollam@gmail.com",
                        "username": "abhilashraju"
                    },
                    "message": "We are not using the SPDM 1.2 SET_CERTIFICATE message for certificate provisioning. The naming can be confusing; we are considering renaming this service to something like \"Bmcgroup\" to clarify its purpose.\n\nThe actual use case is an attestation and trust establishment process within a network of BMCs:\n1) Attestation (SPDM Used Here): The primary BMC acts as an SPDM Requester/Verifier to authenticate a new peer BMC (Responder) using SPDM sessions. This step confirms the peer's genuine identity.\n2) Network Integration (Trust Store Exchange): Once the peer is authenticated via SPDM, the two BMCs exchange certificates for their mTLS (Mutual Transport Layer Security) trust stores.\n3) Secure Communication (Outside SPDM): This trust store exchange ensures that all subsequent high-level communications (HTTPS, TCP, etc.) between the BMCs happen over a trusted, secure channel. These communications operate independently of the underlying SPDM channel.\n\nIn summary, SPDM is used purely for attestation of the peer device, and the \"provisioning\" refers to configuring the network-level trust relationship, not using the SPDM 1.2 SET_CERTIFICATE command itself. \n\nI will update the design doc once we have some better clarity on right name."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Provisioning/Provisioning.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "This implies there is only 1 peer.  Why are we doing that?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Provisioning/Provisioning.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Abhilash Raju",
                        "email": "abhilash.kollam@gmail.com",
                        "username": "abhilashraju"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes now it assumes single peer. Ideally PeerConnected,ProvisionPeer, and PeerProvisioned should go under Peer object."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Provisioning/Provisioning.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 30,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "what is an \"identifier\"?  We generally do not want arbitrary strings.  Is there some format of these?  Is it an IP address?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Provisioning/Provisioning.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 30,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Abhilash Raju",
                        "email": "abhilash.kollam@gmail.com",
                        "username": "abhilashraju"
                    },
                    "message": "The current plan is to use component integrity ID exposed by SPDMd."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Provisioning/Provisioning.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 42,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Add an identifier of the peer?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Provisioning/Provisioning.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 42,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Abhilash Raju",
                        "email": "abhilash.kollam@gmail.com",
                        "username": "abhilashraju"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes that is one way to support multiple peers. Another way is to put this under Peer interface. And this interface can host list of peers."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Provisioning/Provisioning.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 64,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Lower case.\n\nPlace this under control or state also."
                }
            ],
